# Theme Token Guidelines

Every visual property in the Stigmer web UI must flow through `--stgm-*` design tokens. This rule covers the concrete patterns that enforce that principle.

## Token Context Awareness

Components render in different visual contexts. Each context has its own token family. Using the wrong family causes theming bugs (e.g. light text on a light background in certain presets).

### Main Content Area

Use the standard tokens: `bg-background`, `text-foreground`, `bg-muted`, `text-muted-foreground`, `bg-card`, `border-border`, etc.

### Sidebar

Use the `sidebar-*` token family exclusively:

| Purpose | Token |
|---------|-------|
| Background | `bg-sidebar` |
| Primary text | `text-sidebar-foreground` |
| Muted/secondary text | `text-sidebar-muted-foreground` |
| Muted background (skeletons, subtle fills) | `bg-sidebar-muted` |
| Hover/active background | `bg-sidebar-accent` |
| Hover/active text | `text-sidebar-accent-foreground` |
| Borders | `border-sidebar-border` |
| Focus ring | `ring-sidebar-ring` |
| Primary accent | `text-sidebar-primary` |

### Portaled Content (Dropdowns, Dialogs, Popovers)

Content inside `DropdownMenuContent`, `DialogContent`, `PopoverContent`, or similar portal components renders **outside** the sidebar DOM tree. These components use their own popover tokens (`bg-popover`, `text-popover-foreground`) or main-area tokens. Do **not** use sidebar tokens inside portaled content.

## No Opacity Modifiers on Design Tokens

Never use Tailwind opacity modifiers (`/60`, `/50`, etc.) on design-token color classes to create a color variant. Each preset needs independent control over these values.

**Wrong:**
```
text-sidebar-foreground/60
bg-sidebar-foreground/10
```

**Right:**
```
text-sidebar-muted-foreground
bg-sidebar-muted
```

If no appropriate token exists for your use case, propose adding one to `sdk/theme/src/tokens.css` rather than using an opacity workaround. The new token must be added to all preset files and the Tailwind bridge in `globals.css`.

## Interactive Elements in Non-Standard Contexts

When placing a button, link, or other interactive component inside the sidebar (or any context with a non-default background), verify its hover, focus, and active states use context-appropriate tokens.

Example: a `variant="ghost"` button inside the sidebar must override its hover styles:

```tsx
<Button
  variant="ghost"
  className="hover:bg-sidebar-accent hover:text-sidebar-accent-foreground"
>
```

The ghost variant's default `hover:bg-muted` would produce a light background on the dark sidebar in Corporate/Fintech themes.

## Verification

After making changes to web UI components, run `make lint` to verify theme token compliance. The custom `eslint-plugin-stigmer` rules will flag:

- **`stigmer/no-main-tokens-in-sidebar`** — main-area tokens used in sidebar-context files
- **`stigmer/no-token-opacity-modifiers`** — opacity modifiers on design-token classes

`make check` runs the full CI gate, which includes these lint rules.
